{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And one shall descend from Jacob.</b> Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said that he is speaking about Yoav. And Rashi, of blessed memory, interpreted it about King Messiah, and his words are primary — for according to the words of Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, he should have placed this statement before the statement ״וְהָיָה אֱדוֹם יְרֵשָׁה״, which concerns the Messiah. And according to the words of Rashi, of blessed memory, even though he already said ״וְקַרְקַר כָּל בְּנֵי שֵׁת״ and included all who descended from Seth son of the first man, he comes to say that even though some of the nations will survive after ״וְקַרְקַר״ etc. — as is also evident from the implication of ״וְקַרְקַר״, that he will not destroy all of them, as stated by our Sages, of blessed memory (Pesachim 68b) — nevertheless there will be no rulership from them but only from Jacob. And thus his saying <b>וְיֵרְדְּ</b> — meaning, an expression of kingship — <b>מִיַּעֲקֹב</b>, meaning, from Jacob alone it shall be." "And his saying <b>וְהֶאֱבִיד שָׂרִיד מֵעִיר</b>, meaning: not a king alone — that he would be from Jacob and not from them — but even a שָׂרִיד, which is a ruler below the level of kingship, shall perish from them, and he will never again raise up a head from them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:00.523557681Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}